[![Ordered quantities are partitioned across fulfilment outcomes.](lessons/lld-19-flow.svg)](lessons/lld-19-flow.svg)

## Closing answer

"An Order holds OrderLines – product, seller, quantity and unit price – and each line splits its units into pending, shipped and cancelled, with returns tracked against shipped units, so a unit is never counted twice. Customers can cancel only pending units, and the refund is units times price. Pending units are grouped by seller into Shipments, each with its own delivery status. The order's status – placed, partly shipped, shipped, delivered or cancelled – is calculated from the lines and shipments rather than stored, so it can't contradict them. Across services, cancelling and packing use a conditional update on the line so only one can take the last unit."
